<Base title="Designing for a connection that can fail — Evan Han"><article class="article"><a class="back" href="/#notes">← Back to field notes</a><div class="eyebrow">CONNECTIVITY / TCP/IP</div><h1>Designing for a connection that can fail</h1><p class="notice">An independent learning note using a hypothetical device. No company systems or data are described.</p><p>A network connection is useful, but it is not a guarantee that a device is ready or that a complete message has arrived. A small communication client should make those distinctions visible.</p><h2>Start with message boundaries</h2><p>TCP delivers a stream of bytes, not a stream of complete application messages. Define a framing strategy—such as a length prefix or a delimiter—and buffer partial input until a complete message is available. Validate lengths and reject malformed data.</p><h2>Make state explicit</h2><p>Model connecting, connected, disconnected, and reconnecting as distinct states. Use timeouts, bounded retries and backoff; allow an operator to see when data is stale. A successful socket connection alone does not prove a device has accepted a command.</p><h2>Keep the UI responsive</h2><p>Perform network operations asynchronously, propagate cancellation, and marshal updates to the UI thread. Test with a local simulator that fragments messages, delays replies, and closes the connection unexpectedly.</p></article></Base>

